---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap: How to Choose';
const description = 'Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap (Medicare Supplement): how they differ, who each suits, and how to choose — plus why switching to Medigap is easier in New York and Connecticut.';
const today = '2026-06-03';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the difference between Medicare Advantage and Medigap?', a: 'Medicare Advantage is a private plan that replaces how you receive Original Medicare, usually bundling coverage with a provider network and extra benefits. Medigap works alongside Original Medicare, paying the deductibles, copays, and coinsurance it leaves behind, and lets you use any provider nationwide that accepts Medicare. You generally choose one approach or the other.' },
  { q: 'Can you have both Medicare Advantage and Medigap at the same time?', a: 'No. Medigap is designed to work with Original Medicare, not with a Medicare Advantage plan, and it cannot be used to cover Medicare Advantage cost-sharing. You choose one path at a time.' },
  { q: 'Is Medigap better than Medicare Advantage?', a: 'Neither is better in the abstract — they suit different priorities. Medigap tends to fit people who value provider freedom and predictable costs; Medicare Advantage tends to fit people who want a low premium and bundled extras and are comfortable with a network. The right choice depends on your health, budget, and preferences.' },
  { q: 'Can I switch from Medicare Advantage to Medigap?', a: 'Often, yes, but timing and health matter. In New York and Connecticut you can move to a Medigap plan any time of year with no health questions. In most other states, switching to Medigap after your initial window usually requires medical underwriting, and approval is not guaranteed.' },
  { q: 'Which is cheaper, Medicare Advantage or Medigap?', a: 'It depends on how much care you use. Medicare Advantage usually has a lower premium but more out-of-pocket cost when you use care; Medigap has a higher premium but more predictable costs. Comparing your total expected annual cost — not just the premium — is the way to know.' },
];

const articleSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: 'Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap: How to Choose', description, author: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#agent` }, publisher: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, datePublished: today, dateModified: today, mainEntityOfPage: `${BUSINESS.url}/medicare-advantage-vs-medigap` };
const faqSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) };
const speakableSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/medicare-advantage-vs-medigap#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } };
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': [articleSchema, faqSchema, speakableSchema] };
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap"
    subtitle="Two very different ways to round out Medicare. Here’s how they compare, who each one suits, and how to decide — explained plainly and fairly."
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Medicare Plans', href: '/services' }, { label: 'Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap' }]}
  />

  <div class="bg-crst-light border-b border-navy-100">
    <div class="container-wide py-3 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-2 sm:gap-4 text-center">
      <span class="text-sm font-heading text-navy-700">Want help weighing the two for your situation?</span>
      <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-sm px-5 py-2">Call or text {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <div class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-crst-gold bg-navy-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-navy-800 leading-relaxed m-0">
          <strong>Medicare Advantage and Medigap are two different ways to fill the gaps in Original Medicare — and you generally
          choose one or the other, not both.</strong> Medicare Advantage bundles your coverage through a private plan, usually with a
          network and extra benefits. Medigap pairs with Original Medicare to cover its out-of-pocket costs and lets you use any
          provider that accepts Medicare. The right choice depends on how you weigh flexibility, predictability, and cost.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        This is one of the biggest decisions in Medicare, and there’s no universally “right” answer — only the right answer for you.
        Let’s lay out both honestly.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 -mt-2">
        Written by <a href="/about">Jim Farnham, MS, MBA</a> — licensed independent agent (NY license {BUSINESS.nyLicense}).
      </p>

      <h2>What’s the difference between Medicare Advantage and Medigap?</h2>
      <p>
        The simplest way to see it: Medicare Advantage <em>replaces</em> how you get your Medicare benefits, while Medigap
        <em>supplements</em> Original Medicare. With Advantage, a private plan administers your coverage. With Medigap, you keep
        Original Medicare and add a policy that pays the costs Medicare leaves to you. They take opposite approaches to the same goal
        — protecting you from large medical bills.
      </p>

      <h2>How Medicare Advantage (Part C) works</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare Advantage plans are offered by private insurers approved by Medicare. They usually bundle hospital, medical, and
        often drug coverage into one plan, and many include extras such as dental, vision, or hearing. Premiums are frequently low,
        but you typically pay copays or coinsurance as you use care, up to an annual out-of-pocket maximum. Most plans use a provider
        network, may require referrals, and can change their benefits, costs, and networks each year. You can learn more on our
        <a href="/services/medicare-advantage">Medicare Advantage page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How Medigap (Medicare Supplement) works</h2>
      <p>
        A Medigap plan works alongside Original Medicare, paying the deductibles, copays, and coinsurance it leaves to you. There are
        no networks and no referrals — you can see any doctor or hospital in the country that accepts Medicare. Costs are more
        predictable, benefits are standardized by law, and the plan doesn’t change year to year the way Advantage plans can. Medigap
        does not include drug coverage, so most people add a separate <a href="/services/prescription-drug-plans">Part D plan</a>.
        See our <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">Medicare Supplement overview</a> for the details.
      </p>

      <h2>Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap, side by side</h2>
      <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-5">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-navy-100 bg-navy-50 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-primary mb-3 mt-0">Medicare Advantage</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-navy-600 m-0 list-none p-0">
            <li>• Often a low monthly premium</li>
            <li>• Bundled coverage, frequently with extras (dental, vision, hearing)</li>
            <li>• Network-based; may need referrals</li>
            <li>• Copays/coinsurance as you go, up to a yearly maximum</li>
            <li>• Benefits and networks can change each year</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-5 text-white">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-gold mb-3 mt-0">Medigap (Medicare Supplement)</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-white/85 m-0 list-none p-0">
            <li>• Higher monthly premium, more predictable costs</li>
            <li>• Any provider nationwide that accepts Medicare</li>
            <li>• No networks, no referrals</li>
            <li>• Standardized benefits that don’t change yearly</li>
            <li>• Drug coverage is separate (add Part D)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Which one is right for you?</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare Advantage tends to suit people who want a low premium, like the idea of bundled extras, and are comfortable working
        within a network. Medigap tends to suit people who travel or want to keep their choice of doctors, who prefer predictable
        costs, and who don’t want to re-evaluate their plan every year. Many people weigh how much they value provider freedom
        against a lower premium — there’s no wrong answer, only the one that fits your life.
      </p>

      <h2>Can you switch between Medicare Advantage and Medigap?</h2>
      <p>
        You can, but the rules differ by direction and state. Moving from Medigap into a Medicare Advantage plan generally happens
        during set enrollment periods. Moving from Medicare Advantage back to Medigap is where state law matters most: in most
        states it can require medical underwriting after your initial window, and you could be declined. In
        <a href="/new-york-medicare-supplement">New York</a> and <a href="/connecticut-medicare-supplement">Connecticut</a>, you can
        move to Medigap any time of year with no health questions — a meaningful advantage if you ever want to change course.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-3 m-0">Not sure which path fits you?</p>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mb-4 m-0">I’ll walk you through both, honestly — no cost, no pressure.</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <h2>What this means in New York and Connecticut</h2>
      <p>
        Because New York and Connecticut offer year-round, guaranteed-issue Medigap, residents there have unusual freedom: you can
        choose Medigap now and switch later without underwriting, or move from a Medicare Advantage plan into Medigap in any month.
        That flexibility makes Medigap far more accessible than it is in most states — see the
        <a href="/new-york-medicare-supplement">New York Medicare Supplement overview</a> for how it works.
      </p>

      <h2>Where High-Deductible Plan G fits</h2>
      <p>
        If a Medigap premium feels high but you like the freedom and predictability, there’s a middle path:
        <a href="/services/high-deductible-medicare-supplement">High-Deductible Plan G</a> keeps Medigap’s any-provider freedom at a
        much lower premium, in exchange for a higher annual deductible. It’s often overlooked in this comparison, and it’s worth
        knowing about before you decide.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((f) => (<div class="mb-5"><h3 class="text-xl">{f.q}</h3><p>{f.a}</p></div>))}

      <AuthorByline />

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          This page is educational and compares two types of coverage in general terms; specific plan benefits, costs, networks, and
          availability vary by plan and location and are not guaranteed. Enrollment and switching rules reflect general and New
          York/Connecticut law as of 2026 and may change. NY producer license #: {BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          Source: <a href="https://www.medicare.gov" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov</a> — official information on Medicare Advantage, Medigap, and Part D.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="Medicare Advantage or Medigap? Let’s find your fit." />
</BaseLayout>
